// Regression fixture for final-review Critical 1 (memory-audit-4e
// whole-branch review, probe P5): an element of one global container read
// into another global. Originally (4e): `cl_free_globals` had no runtime-
// aliasing analysis, so it always froze both `p5Gl` (element-freeing its
// one stored handle) and `p5GSaved` (freeing the very same handle again):
// SIGSEGV. The globals escape pre-pass fixed the crash by disqualifying
// both from `cl_free_globals` -- still true today (Task 6's pass, untouched
// by ARC Task 4): both `p5Gl` and `p5GSaved` are globals, so NEITHER gets a
// scope-exit release at all (that only ever applies to locals) -- their
// only possible release is `cl_free_globals`, which stays empty.
//
// ARC Task 4 (counted named-slot stores): `p5GSaved = p5Gl[0]` is a
// NAMED-SLOT store (global) whose source is a non-birth list-index read
// (`EIndexRef`, a raw borrow -- see p4_elem_escape.cla's own comment for
// why that's different from a map subscript) -- `lowCountedStore` retains
// it FIRST, then releases `p5GSaved`'s OLD (default-empty) handle: a real
// free now, not the permanent orphan-on-overwrite this comment used to
// describe (confirmed against the emitted C: `rt_text_retain(cv___store1);
// rt_text_release(cv_p5GSaved); cv_p5GSaved = cv___store1;`). Expected
// strict-mode leak count: 4 (see ../p5_global_elem_alias.leaks, dropped
// from 6) -- `p5Gl`'s own list (struct box + backing-array handle, 2
// blocks; a global, never released -- Task 6's still-unmigrated slot class)
// plus the one `"x"+"y"` text handle it holds (2 blocks, shared with
// `p5GSaved`'s now-live retained reference, counted once; also never
// released, same reason). Not a bug -- global cleanup is explicitly out of
// this task's scope, staged for Task 6.

var p5Gl: list of text
var p5GSaved: text

func runP5GlobalElemAlias() {
    p5Gl.push("x" + "y")
    p5GSaved = p5Gl[0]
    alert("p5 done")
}
